// Pull request author: Diablo-research
// Change: new rule
// Title: Suspicious Child Process From IEDiagCmd.EXE
// Sigma ID: 51841f84-6bac-4405-848d-87df4b91fe32
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Nazar Diachok (@Diablo-research)
// Date: 2026-09-29 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1218
// Description: Detects IEDiagCmd.exe spawning a child process from outside the standard Windows system
//   directories. Attackers can redirect the WINDIR environment variable to make IEDiagCmd.exe
//   execute a pre-planted binary such as netsh.exe from a user-controlled location.
// False positives:
//   - Custom diagnostic workflows that intentionally cause IEDiagCmd.exe to launch a process outside the standard Windows system directories
// References:
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/Binaries/Iediagcmd/
//   - https://twitter.com/Hexacorn/status/1507516393859731456
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/f3ce0ac3356d71d8e6c6d8d5f544c07750c7ec12/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_iediagcmd_susp_child.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\iediagcmd.exe" and (not((FolderPath contains ":\\Windows\\System32\\" or FolderPath contains ":\\Windows\\SysWOW64\\")))
